1.1  What does the kernel do?

TL;DR
  - The kernel is the one program with full control of the hardware. It shares
    the CPUs, the memory and the devices among all the others.
  - Programs run in user space and can't touch the hardware: they ask the
    kernel, through system calls (21.4).
  - Linux is one big program, a monolithic kernel, that loads most drivers as
    modules when needed (21.2).

      uname -r              # the running kernel
      cat /proc/version     # who built it, and with what

On your machine
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64, built with gcc 14.2.0
  Build     #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Debian 6.12.48-1 (2025-09-20)
  Work      in the 3 days, 4 h since boot: 912.3 million context switches (3,330
            per second), 1.2 million processes started, 51.2 million interrupts
  Now       3 tasks running or ready, 1 waiting on I/O
  Disk FS   ext4, vfat, btrfs

Pitfalls
  - "Linux" is only the kernel: the shell, systemd and the desktop come from
    other projects, and a distribution puts them together.
  - The kernel's own threads show in ps between brackets: [kswapd0],
    [kworker/0:1]. They aren't programs you can uninstall.
  - A bug in a program kills that program; a bug in the kernel stops the whole
    machine (a "kernel panic"). That's the price of full control.
  - A new kernel only runs after a reboot.

Deep dive

  ▾ What separates user space from kernel space?

    The CPU itself. It has privilege levels: kernel code runs at the highest
    (ring 0 on x86, EL1 on ARM) and may touch any memory and any device;
    programs run at the lowest (ring 3, EL0). When a program tries a privileged
    instruction, or memory it doesn't own, the CPU stops it and the kernel
    usually ends it, with a "segmentation fault". The only door between the two
    sides is the system call.

  ▾ Why one big kernel rather than a microkernel?

    In a microkernel (Minix, seL4), drivers and filesystems run as separate
    programs: a bug in one can't bring the others down, but every request
    crosses more boundaries. Linux keeps everything in one address space for
    speed, and gets its flexibility from modules. The famous 1992 debate between
    Andrew Tanenbaum and Linus Torvalds was about exactly this.

  ▾ Why is everything a file?

    The kernel offers one interface, open, read, write, close, for files,
    devices, pipes, sockets and its own information. A layer called the VFS
    (virtual filesystem) sits under every filesystem and makes that work. That's
    why cat reads /proc/meminfo and /dev/null as easily as a text file.

